WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:02.020
- Every law student is terrified

00:00:02.020 --> 00:00:04.210
of being cold called by
the professor in class

00:00:04.210 --> 00:00:06.761
and as a result they spend so much time

00:00:06.761 --> 00:00:09.300
reading their cases over and over again

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:10.830
and studying on the off-chance

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:12.682
that they might get called on.

00:00:12.682 --> 00:00:15.030
But there is a better way.

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:18.240
So use this trick to
supercharge your studying,

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:21.020
to make sure that you are
more prepared for the final

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:23.860
and are prepared for
any cold call question

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:25.647
your professor may give you.

00:00:25.647 --> 00:00:28.314
(twinkly music)

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:33.800
Every law student is afraid
of being called on in class.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:36.740
Being on the hotseat and being
cold called by the professor

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:38.600
can be a terrifying experience

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:41.480
and as a result we tend
to spend so much time

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:43.300
preparing for class and reading

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:44.867
and re-reading our cases.

00:00:44.867 --> 00:00:46.320
But you know what?

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:47.520
There's a better way.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.970
So use this trick to
supercharge your studying

00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:52.940
in a way that will actually
help you on the final exam

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:54.860
but will also prepare you for class

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:56.490
so that you can answer any question

00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:57.850
your professor throws at you.

00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:00.350
Okay, so here at Legal Eagle we call this

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:03.740
the flipped case method and here's why.

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:05.580
The normal way of studying is this.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:07.600
You struggle through reading a case.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:11.070
The average reading speed
is about 10 pages per hour.

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:13.210
You try to figure out the point of a case.

00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:15.800
Then when you think you have
it you re-read the case.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:18.400
Then you write a long brief
including the parties,

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.500
the procedural posture,
the trial accord decision,

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:22.830
the facts, disposition,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:24.455
You write all of this crap down

00:01:24.455 --> 00:01:26.440
just on the off-chance that your professor

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:29.440
happens to ask you
about some random detail

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:31.210
about the particular case.

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:33.440
Then maybe you go read
a commercial outline

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:35.290
or a supplement, if you're lucky

00:01:35.290 --> 00:01:38.390
and then you just go to
class and hope for the best.

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:41.040
That is a bad way to study for class.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:42.680
Number one, it's totally inefficient.

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:45.150
You're starting with the
really hard stuff first

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:47.750
and then reading the summaries later.

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:50.930
On top of that, you're
focusing on all of the material

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:53.540
that is not going to help
you on the final exam.

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:56.820
In our other videos on case
briefing, links down below

00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:59.621
we talk about how so much time is wasted

00:01:59.621 --> 00:02:01.620
on a standard case brief

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:04.210
because almost none of that information

00:02:04.210 --> 00:02:06.610
is going to be tested on the final exam.

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:10.270
Final exam is about applying
the facts to the law,

00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:14.620
not about the rationale
or the procedural posture

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:17.600
of some random case from New Hampshire.

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:19.380
What we recommend instead is this.

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:21.560
It's the flipped case method.

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:25.340
The idea is, start with
the supplement first.

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:27.330
That's right, you read your supplements

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:30.350
and your summaries
before you read the case.

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:33.610
Read the summary first,
several of them if you can

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:36.220
and only then, once you have a general

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:38.460
or sometimes even a very specific idea

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:39.630
of what the case is about.

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:42.870
Only then, you go into the case itself.

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:46.605
So, for example if you happen
to have an outline for course

00:02:46.605 --> 00:02:50.050
and it happens to be keyed
to your particular casebook

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:53.330
go directly to the summary of
the case you're about to read

00:02:53.330 --> 00:02:55.230
and read the summary first.

00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:56.440
Or you can use Westlaw.

00:02:56.440 --> 00:02:59.040
Westlaw is an entirely underrated
resource in law school.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:01.250
They have a whole army of attorneys

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:05.120
who are summarizing the
most important facts

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:08.160
and black letter law of a particular case.

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:11.140
Or you can go to an old
upper classman's outline.

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:12.810
Or you can go to Quimby.

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:16.650
The trick is to get an idea
of the point of the case

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:18.610
before you start delving into it.

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:20.630
One person said that reading a case

00:03:20.630 --> 00:03:23.740
is like stirring cement
with your eyelashes.

00:03:23.740 --> 00:03:26.210
It just goes so slowly.

00:03:26.210 --> 00:03:29.790
But if you know what you're
looking for ahead of time,

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:31.740
then once you start reading the case,

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:33.460
you can go through it more quickly.

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:35.440
Now, I wouldn't skim through the case

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:37.970
but I would certainly go
faster than you normally read

00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:40.490
because you know exactly
what you're looking for.

00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:43.150
Pay special attention
to the particular facts,

00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:45.080
the narrative of the story

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:48.440
but most importantly pay
attention to the black letter law

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:51.410
which you probably already have
in the form of the summaries

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:53.370
in the outlines and the
commercial supplements

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:54.310
that you've already read.

00:03:54.310 --> 00:03:56.590
Of course not all cases are created equal.

00:03:56.590 --> 00:03:59.240
Some are important only
for a particular statement

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:01.260
of black letter law and you
might not never even learn

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:02.850
the facts themselves.

00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:06.100
Some are important only for
the outcome based on the facts.

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:08.950
Some cases are only important
for the historical value

00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:10.750
and you're never going to actually use

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:13.780
the facts or the black
letter law in that case.

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:16.341
But the point is in any of
those particular scenarios

00:04:16.341 --> 00:04:20.010
it's better to have an idea
of what you are looking for

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:21.910
before you delve into the case.

00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:24.690
You'll retain more and
you'll struggle less

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:27.260
and on top of that you can skip the long,

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:29.860
meandering rainbow case brief too.

